'''Lake Maggiore''' ({{IPAc-en|UK|m|æ|ˈ|dʒ|ɔːr|eɪ|,_|ˌ|m|æ|dʒ|i|ˈ|ɔːr|eɪ}}, {{IPAc-en|US|m|ɑː|ˈ|dʒ|ɔːr|eɪ|,_|m|ə|ˈ|dʒ|ɔːr|i}};<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.collinsdictionary.com/dictionary/english/maggiore|title=Maggiore|work=[[Collins English Dictionary]]|publisher=[[HarperCollins]]|access-date=28 May 2019}}</ref><ref>[https://web.archive.org/web/20190528120853/https://en.oxforddictionaries.com/definition/us/Maggiore,_Lake "Maggiore, Lake"] (US) and {{Cite encyclopedia |url=http://www.lexico.com/definition/Maggiore,+Lake |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20210416232904/https://www.lexico.com/definition/maggiore,_lake?s=t |url-status=dead |archive-date=2021-04-16 |title=Maggiore, Lake |dictionary=[[Lexico]] UK English Dictionary |publisher=[[Oxford University Press]]}}</ref><ref>{{Cite Merriam-Webster|Maggiore, Lake|access-date=28 May 2019}}</ref> {{langx|it|Lago Maggiore}} {{IPA|it|ˈlaːɡo madˈdʒoːre}}; {{langx|lmo|label=[[Western Lombard]]|Lagh Maggior}}; {{langx|pms|Lagh Magior}}; literally 'greater lake') or '''Verbano''' ({{IPA|it|verˈbaːno|pron}}; {{langx|la|Lacus Verbanus}}) is a large [[lake]] on the south side of the [[Alps]] divided between the Italian regions of [[Piedmont]] and [[Lombardy]] to the south, and the Swiss canton of [[Ticino]] to the north. Located halfway between [[Lake Orta]] and [[Lake Lugano]], Lake Maggiore extends for about {{convert|64|km|0|abbr=off}} between [[Locarno]] and [[Arona (NO)|Arona]]. It is the [[List of lakes of Italy|second largest]] lake in [[Italy]] and the [[List of lakes of Switzerland|largest]] in southern [[Switzerland]].  

The climate is mild in both summer and winter, producing [[Mediterranean]] vegetation, with many gardens growing rare and exotic plants. Well-known gardens include those of the [[Borromean Islands|Borromean]] and [[Brissago Islands]], that of the [[Villa Taranto]] in [[Verbania]], and the [[Alpinia Botanical Garden]] above [[Stresa]].

Lake Maggiore is drained by the river [[Ticino (river)|Ticino]], a main tributary of the [[Po (river)|Po]]. Its basin also collects the waters of several large lakes, notably Lake Lugano (through the [[Tresa]]), Lake Orta (through the [[Toce]]) and [[Lake Varese]] (through the Bardello).<ref>[[Swisstopo]] topographic maps.</ref>

==Geography==
Lake Maggiore is {{convert|64.37|km|0|abbr=on}} long, and {{convert|3|to|5|km|0|abbr=on}} wide, except at the bay opening westward between [[Pallanza]] and [[Stresa]], where it is {{convert|10|km|0|abbr=on}} wide. It is the longest Italian lake, and second to [[Lake Garda]] in area. Its mean height above the sea level is {{convert|193|m|0|abbr=on}} ; a deep lake, its bottom is almost everywhere below sea-level: at its deepest, {{convert|179|m|0|abbr=on}}  below. Its form is very sinuous so that there are few points from which any considerable part of its surface can be seen at a single glance. If this lessens the effect of the apparent size, it increases the variety of its scenery. While the upper end is completely [[Alps|alpine]] in character, the middle region lies between hills of gentler form, and the lower end advances to the verge of the plain of [[Lombardy]].<ref>this paragraph is taken largely verbatim from [[John Ball (naturalist)|John Ball]], ''The Alpine Guide, Central Alps'', 1856, p. 306</ref> Lake Maggiore is the most westerly of the three great southern [[Prealps|prealpine]] lakes, the others being [[Lake Como]] and Lake Garda.

[[File:Rifugioallegn.jpg|thumb|left|Upper lake with the [[Brissago Islands]] and Maggia delta from above [[Brissago]], [[Switzerland]]]]
The lake basin has tectonic-glacial origins and its volume is {{convert|37|km³|0|abbr=out}}.{{citation needed|date=November 2022}} The lake has a surface area of about {{convert|213|km2|0|abbr=out}}, a maximum length of {{convert|54|km|0|abbr=on}} (on a straight line) and, at its widest, is {{convert|12|km|0|abbr=on}}.{{citation needed|date=November 2022}} Its main tributaries are the [[Ticino (river)|Ticino]], the [[Maggia (river)|Maggia]] (forming a very large delta), the [[Toce]] (by which it receives the outflow of [[Lake Orta]]) and the [[Tresa]] (which is the sole emissary of [[Lake Lugano]]). The rivers [[Verzasca (river)|Verzasca]], [[Giona (torrent)|Giona]], and [[Cannobino]] also flow into the lake. Its outlet is the Ticino which, in turn, joins the river [[Po (river)|Po]] just south-east of [[Pavia]].

The lake's jagged banks are surrounded by the [[Pennine Alps|Pennine]] and [[Lepontine Alps]], and [[Lugano Prealps]]. Prominent peaks around the lake are the [[Gridone]], [[Monte Tamaro]], [[Monte Nudo]] and the [[Mottarone]]. The highest mountain overlooking Lake Maggiore is [[Monte Rosa]] ({{convert|4634|m|abbr=on|disp=semicolon}}), about {{convert|50|km}} west of it. The western bank is in [[Piedmont]] (provinces of [[Province of Novara|Novara]] and [[Province of Verbano-Cusio-Ossola]]) and the eastern in [[Lombardy]] ([[province of Varese]]), whereas the most northerly section extends {{convert|13|km|0}} into the canton of [[Ticino]], where it constitutes [[Extreme points of Switzerland|its lowest point]] [[AMSL|above sea level]] as well as that of [[Switzerland]]. The culminating point of the lake's [[drainage basin]] is the [[Grenzgipfel]] summit of Monte Rosa at {{convert|4618|m}} above sea level.<ref>{{cite map |publisher= [[Swisstopo]] |title= 1:25,000 topographic map |url= http://s.geo.admin.ch/1c2b73661 |access-date= 2014-07-28 }}</ref>

===Climate===
{{See also|Italian Lakes#Climate}}
Lake Maggiore weather is [[humid subtropical]] (''Cfa'' in the [[Köppen climate classification]]). During winter, the lake helps to maintain a higher temperature in the surrounding region (since water releases heat energy more slowly than air). The temperatures are cooled down in summer by the breezes that blow on the water's surface, changing its colour. The area enjoys nearly 2300 hours of sunshine a year and an average annual temperature of {{convert|15.5|°C|0|abbr=on}}. The water of the lake has a comfortable temperature of {{convert|20|to|22|°C|0|abbr=on}} in July and August. In winter snowfall is erratic and primarily affects the higher elevations. Rainfall is heaviest in May and lowest during the winter months.<ref>[http://www.lagomaggioreonline.it/history_lake_maggiore.htm The history of Lake Maggiore] {{Webarchive|url=https://web.archive.org/web/20160613122554/http://www.lagomaggioreonline.it/history_lake_maggiore.htm |date=2016-06-13 }} lagomaggioreonline.it. Retrieved 2010-03-12</ref><ref>[http://www.myswitzerland.com/en/interests/adventure_sports/swiss-waters/lake-lago-maggiore.html Lake Maggiore] myswitzerland.com. Retrieved 2010-03-12</ref><ref>[http://www.travel-swiss.co.uk/lakes/lago-maggiore.html Lago maggiore - a popular holiday destination in Switzerland as well] {{Webarchive|url=https://web.archive.org/web/20130923060655/http://www.travel-swiss.co.uk/lakes/lago-maggiore.html |date=2013-09-23 }} travel-swiss.co.uk. Retrieved 2010-03-12</ref>

=== Flora and fauna ===
The flora is strongly influenced by the lake basin, which has allowed the proliferation of typically Mediterranean plants, and also of plants native to the Atlantic areas favoured by the composition of the soil and the abundance of siliceous rocks. [[Lemons]], [[olive trees]] and bay olive trees grow there.{{citation needed|date=November 2022}} The spontaneous vegetation is composed of [[Taxus|yew]], holly and chestnut trees on the surrounding hills.

The lake is a habitat to two species of whitefish, ''[[Coregonus]]'' and, less widespread, ''[[Coregonus lavaretus]]''. Both live in deep water and come ashore only during the spawning in early December. There are also [[perch]], pike, [[chub]], [[burbot]], torpedo, [[eel]]s and ''[[Alburnus arborella]]''. The lake is home to several species of nesting waterfowl, it also represents an important corridor, a place of rest and feeding for migrations. For example: [[common merganser]], [[mute swan]], [[grebes]], [[gull]]s, [[Great cormorant|cormorants]], [[duck]]s.<ref>{{Cite web|title=NUMEROSI UCCELLI ACQUATICI SUL LAGO MAGGIORE|url=http://www.lagomaggiorelife.it/?p=645|url-status=live|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20130729145659/http://www.lagomaggiorelife.it/?p=645|archive-date=2013-07-29}}</ref>

A number of exotic species have established themselves in the lake, including [[pikeperch]], which has been recorded since 1977; [[wels catfish]], which was first noticed in the early 1990s; and [[ruffe]], introduced in the mid-1990s. Wels catfish in excess of 50&nbsp;kg in weight have been fished from the lake.<ref name="Volta 2017">{{cite journal | last1 = Volta | first1 = Pietra | last2 = Jeppesen | first2 = Erik | last3 = Sala | first3 = Paolo | last4 = Silvia | first4 = Galafassi | last5 = Foglini | first5 = Claudio | last6 = Puzzi | first6 = Cesare | last7 = Winfield | first7 = Ian J. | date = 10 July 2017 | title = Fish assemblages in large Italian subalpine lakes: history and present status with an emphasis on non-native species | url = https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/s10750-018-3621-0 | journal = Hydrobiologia | volume = 824 | issue = 1 | pages = 255–270 | doi = 10.1007/s10750-018-3621-0 | s2cid = 254544327 | access-date = March 10, 2023| url-access = subscription }}</ref>

